  },
  "holistic_F_N": 2,
  "holistic_F_H": -2,
  "section_notes": "The final S2 keeps a 5% action threshold but explicitly removes any automatic sales stop, so the Hearth 10 (6.2%) can stay on sale, while exact per-model rates go to regulators and to owners and prospective customers, with no public release. S3's six-year retrofit deadline favours Northwind's low remote shut-off coverage (38%), and S4's 36-month suppression start favours Halcyon's cost position. S5 makes an 8-per-100k mandatory recall trigger with no transition, which applies to Halcyon's Ember 8 at 9.9, so it weighs against Halcyon."
 },
